    "narrative": "The offensive outlook for LawDog today is anchored by **Dylan Beavers** (69.52), **Geraldo Perdomo** (69.26), and **Bryce Harper** (68.70), who all benefit significantly from favorable park factors and exploitable matchups against mid-tier starters. **Dylan Beavers** stands out as the premier play; he enjoys a massive 16% park adjustment against Reynaldo L\u00f3pez, and his position atop the order provides the volume necessary to capitalize on a high-scoring environment. Similarly, **Geraldo Perdomo**\u2019s high efficiency score is driven by a favorable matchup against Miles Mikolas, where his #2 spot in the order maximizes his run-scoring potential. **Bryce Harper** remains our safest high-ceiling option, maintaining a robust 0.381 xwOBA and benefitting from the reliability of hitting third against Will Warren.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today was excluding **Ryan Waldschmidt** (53.13) from the starting nine. While his score is respectable, he ultimately fell short due to his projected #9 spot in the batting order\u2014a significant drag compared to the top-of-the-order security offered by **Jeremiah Jackson** (65.72) and **Taylor Ward** (64.26). Both **Jeremiah Jackson** and **Taylor Ward** are prime beneficiaries of the same park boost elevating **Dylan Beavers**, making them more efficient options for category accumulation. We are proceeding with caution regarding **Cal Raleigh** (43.30), whose floor is dangerously low today due to a brutal matchup against Jacob deGrom. The elite nature of deGrom mandates a -19% SP skill penalty, severely suppressing **Cal Raleigh**'s upside; he remains in the lineup only as a volume play, but he is a candidate for early benching if his game count is nearing a ceiling. Finally, please note that all lineups remain pending; any shift in the batting order, particularly for leadoff hitters like **Luis Arraez** or **Dylan Beavers**, could necessitate a re-evaluation of our daily projected output.",
